{"version":1,"entries":{"Narrator: High up on Antuco Mountain, Carlos kicks his red rubber ball so hard that it flies high in the sky like a shooting star. Far across the world, Ashanti is playing Da Ga with her friends. It's Ashanti's turn to be Da Ga, the boa constrictor, so she goes into the center of the circle. Her friends dance and skip around her while she acts like a snake and tries to tag the next Da Ga. It's fun to catch them! Ashanti is tagging a friend when something red drops out of the sky into the circle.\n\nAshanti: Where did this come from? There is no ball in Da Ga!\n\nNarrator: Ashanti laughs. She bounces the ball as hard as she can. It flies high and fast across the sky until she can't see it anymore. It flies over a forest, white cliffs, and a big blue ocean. Far away, Hyo and his brother are playing Omok. Hyo plays with little dark pebbles, and his brother plays with little white ones. Hyo tries to line up five of his stones to win. Winning isn't easy! Hyo is finding the perfect place to put his stone when something red rolls into the middle of the game.\n\nHyo: What's this? You don't need a ball to play Omok!\n\nNarrator: Hyo smiles. Then he throws the ball as hard as he can. It flies far into the sky, over plains and seas. The Fernandez family is playing La Thunkuna. Papa draws boxes in the sand. Mama writes names in the boxes, one for each day of the week. Matias tosses a stone and Maria jumps over it. She lands in the next box without stepping on the line. Maria likes landing on one foot, and she puts her arms out to keep her balance. Something round bounces on her foot.\n\nMaria: Hey! What's this? There is no ball in this game!\n\nNarrator: Everyone laughs. Maria tosses the ball in the air and Matias kicks it up into the sky. The whole family watches as the ball goes up and up until they can't see it anymore. High in the sky, the ball flies over lakes, canyons, and streams. Carlos holds out his arms and catches his red rubber ball.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ad2a460c947717a40fa3.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9cb71a8a6b322e626acd.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Ashanti"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/024562f3c38cd564620f.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ce6e7613f21a7ae1eeec.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Hyo"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/449d030e57402b161cd4.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c6f4cbda90f65cbc4479.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Maria"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/34cfb06b9249a78733bf.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Narrator"}],"Sati (letter to Nisha): July 25th. Dear Nisha, moving is hard work. Mom and I are still unpacking boxes. She and I were packing them just eight weeks ago. Your picture is on my desk. I miss you. Our new house is in a town called St. Cloud, Minnesota. It's very quiet compared to Mumbai. The weather in July is warm and sunny, and there are no rainy monsoons. Sunil and I can play together, but I don't have any friends here. Meeting neighbors isn't easy for me. School starts in September. Dad started his new job as a surgeon at the hospital here. He's happy working here. I'm not happy. I just feel lonely. Please write soon. Your best friend, Sati. October 15th. Dear Nisha, I really miss you. The weather is getting much colder. The leaves are falling from the trees. Sunil and I rake leaves every Saturday in our yard. It's hard work. I go to Maple Hill Elementary School. I'm the only student from India in my class. Today, a boy said that my lunch smelled funny. It was my mom's lentil curry, which is my favorite. I couldn't say anything because I was so angry. I don't like it here. I want to go back to Mumbai. Your best friend, Sati. January 12th. Dear Nisha, it snowed 30 centimeters last night. Today, Sunil and I threw snowballs and went sledding with Emily. Emily is my neighbor and my classmate. We went ice skating last week on a lake. Someone made a hole in the ice and was fishing. It was so cold. Tonight, Emily and I watched one of my favorite Bollywood movies. Mom made curry. Emily liked it. Your best friend, Sati. April 5th. Dear Nisha, happy birthday. I miss you so much. Guess what? I'm taking tap dance lessons with Emily. I'm happy to be dancing again. On Saturday, we went to Minneapolis. Emily came with us. Emily and I went shopping for clothes. We ate at a fun restaurant there. We had so much fun. Emily, Ashley, and Rachel are coming over on Sunday to work on our science project. I'm making some good friends. Your best friend, Sati. June 20th. Dear Nisha, school is over, but I'm getting ready to go to summer camp with Emily. We can go fishing, swimming, hiking, and canoeing there. I'm so excited. The weather is warm and sunny again. When can you visit me in Minnesota? There are so many fun things to do here. My new friends want to meet you too. Please come. Your best friend, Sati.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/283a9f127e736ce02f9b.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Sati (letter to Nisha)"}],"Narrator: People write poems to share what they see. They want us to feel the joy or sadness that they feel. The following three poets come from two countries: the United States and the United Kingdom. However, the language they use is the same: English. Michael Rosen is a British children's book writer and poet. He enjoys writing poetry and reading it aloud with his children. Michael Rosen and his daughter are eating at a table. One day, a raisin escapes! It seems to run away from its jar.\n\nMichael Rosen (poem): A Dangerous Raisin (but my daughter Elsie is helpful). A raisin has escaped from the raisin jar. It's whooshing across the table like a shooting star. Now it's leaping in the air like a kangaroo. Look out, Dad! It's coming for you!\n\nNarrator: Michael Rosen writes very creative poems. His poem Color encourages children to be creative, too.\n\nMichael Rosen (poem): Color. Take a brush. The sky is green. The grass is blue. You are purple. The house is silver. The sun is black. The river is gold. The world has changed. Did you do that?\n\nNarrator: Aileen Fisher was an award-winning American children's poet, book writer, and playwright. She loved nature and liked to go hiking and mountain climbing. Do you like grasshoppers and beetles? Aileen Fisher loved to write about insects.\n\nAileen Fisher (poem): But I Wonder. The crickets in the thickets and the katydids in trees, and ants on plants and butterflies and ladybugs and bees don't smell with little noses, but with feelers, if you please. They get along quite nicely, but I wonder how they sneeze.\n\nNarrator: Robert Louis Stevenson was a famous Scottish poet and book writer who lived in the 1800s. He wrote many books, including the famous Treasure Island. Do you notice how winter days are short and summer days are long? Robert Louis Stevenson noticed it, too, and wrote about it in his poem Bed in Summer.\n\nRobert Louis Stevenson (poem): Bed in Summer. In winter I get up at night and dress by yellow candlelight. In summer, quite the other way, I have to go to bed by day. I have to go to bed and see the birds still hopping on the tree, or hear the grown-up people's feet still going past me in the street. And does it not seem hard to you, when all the sky is clear and blue, and I should like so much to play, to have to go to bed by day?":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6b8dee64d0f47792b089.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/628d7980ac81869e3fe1.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Michael Rosen (poem)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1514d5884e1e7450b91d.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/48ed446e338561735aee.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Michael Rosen (poem)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3f942544caaec8db88b3.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3ad51334c7c26bbade0e.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Aileen Fisher (poem)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fe15133c7a49876cbab3.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5bc72a7e2c0165938285.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Robert Louis Stevenson (poem)"}],"Narrator: Molly's mother was worried. As she packed for her business trip to Japan, Molly stood beside her with a notebook and a pencil.\n\nMolly: Do you have your passport, Mom? she asked.\n\nMom: I do.\n\nMolly: Good, said Molly. Your destination is Tokyo, so you should be at the airport two hours early. Remember, passengers can only check two bags.\n\nMom: Molly, thanks for trying to help, but I'm worried about your father, replied her mother. He isn't feeling well and I have to go on this trip. Please take good care of him while I'm gone.\n\nMolly: I'll be a good doctor and look after him, Mom, just like you look after me, Molly said.\n\nMom: Thank you, said her mother.\n\nNarrator: Soon, Molly's mother left for the airport. The next morning, Molly's father came to the breakfast table. He didn't look well.\n\nMolly: How do you feel, Dad? Molly asked.\n\nDad: I have a bad earache. I think I'm sick, her father answered.\n\nMolly: Then you should take some medicine and go back to bed, said Molly. I'm going to take care of you all day.\n\nDad: I don't think so, Molly. I've got things to do, replied her father.\n\nMolly: Dad, said Molly,Mom put me in charge. I'm the doctor.\n\nDad: Okay, you win. Thank you, Molly, replied her father.\n\nNarrator: Molly looked at the kitchen clock. It was 9 a.m. Should she call her mom? What time was it in Japan? She remembered what her mother told her the day before. Tokyo is 16 hours ahead of Los Angeles. Molly added some numbers in her notebook. It was one o'clock in the morning in Tokyo. Her mom was asleep. Molly sent a text message to her mother.\n\nMolly: Saturday, 9 a.m. Hi, Mom. Dad is sick now. He has an earache, so I put him to bed and gave him medicine. Don't worry, I know you're sleeping. Love, Dr. Molly.\n\nNarrator: Molly's messages continued through the day.\n\nMolly: Saturday, 11 a.m. Hi, Mom. Now Dad has a headache and a fever, so I'm giving him lots of water to drink. I also put a cold washcloth on his forehead. I know it's three in the morning in Tokyo. Don't worry, love, Dr. Molly. Saturday, 2 p.m. Hi, Mom. Dad has a cold now. He's sneezing a lot. He's very noisy. I gave him lots of tissues. It's two p.m. here, so I know it's six in the morning in Tokyo. Love, Dr. Molly.\n\nNarrator: At five in the afternoon, the phone rang. Molly picked it up.\n\nMolly: Hello, this is Dr. Molly speaking.\n\nMom: Molly, it's Mom. How's your dad?\n\nMolly: He's just fine, Mom. His fever is gone, his headache is gone, his earache is gone, and his sneezing is almost gone. He's watching TV and drinking orange juice.\n\nMom: That's great. I was so worried. Good job, Molly, replied her mother.\n\nMolly: You're welcome, Mom. And the cell phone says that it's five p.m. here, so it's nine a.m. in Tokyo. You should eat some breakfast.\n\nMom: Ha, thank you, Molly. And maybe you should study for that science test tomorrow, replied her mother.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/79d5ff59d3ba023a5812.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/87a0664e5a7d21f9f125.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/93cec2435d11250b5fac.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b045b11efbef3aa084d2.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/aff02e39c43cc85e60eb.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0150e7ec17f12d12cec5.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/47b3ff8ab3240801255f.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/327634b697a85762c121.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a3f6dbb237027a69a6e4.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1b73d3e060b9db3eb1e6.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2f7b954e5b56403c116a.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9d40cdb14026c1de1275.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3f6333985a8c94cb3fdd.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/749a19f8ebd4a2d4c4bb.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Dad"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/befcba8077a1a05498d9.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ae7e932b7026cb2f034c.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8e9e9f887e2fa66e3bd0.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/38f556ab7acb30bc0e09.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/cd8a265c10f3c115d3ba.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8bfd752f2870963aee3d.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/14fe36e9f36cd069b2ce.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/7075d4c6c5f661dee5c2.mp3","turn":21,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1d89282d051d18330c0d.mp3","turn":22,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e2419f6009c080a6602a.mp3","turn":23,"speaker":"Molly"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b3c5b23c9b00e957f3e3.mp3","turn":24,"speaker":"Mom"}],"Narrator: Hector Morales was not your normal nine-year-old boy.\n\nMom: Hector, come and eat your breakfast.\n\nHector: But mom, I have to go out and help people save energy.\n\nMom: Eat your breakfast first, young man.\n\nNarrator: The phone rang. It was Mr. Big from the Big Company.\n\nMr. Big: Hector, our company needs to find ways to save energy. Can you help us?\n\nHector: Yes, sir, I'll be there right away.\n\nNarrator: After breakfast, Hector quickly put on his mask and cape, made from old curtains from Uncle Jack's bedroom. He ran outside to his bicycle, Lightning. Lightning was not an ordinary bicycle. It had bells, recycled whistles, and fancy buttons. It had pedals made out of old potato mashers and a seat made from an ironing pad.\n\nHector: Let's help Mr. Big save energy, Lightning.\n\nNarrator: The boy and bicycle sped off into the city. On the way, Hector noticed that something was wrong. A long line of cars, trucks, and taxis stood still on the road. It was a traffic jam. Hector saw an ambulance stuck among the cars. He rode Lightning along the bicycle path and up to the ambulance.\n\nHusband: My wife is going to have a baby.\n\nHector: Have no fear, I can help you.\n\nNarrator: Hector pressed a button on Lightning. Bang, wham, Lightning was now a helicopter. He connected a cable from Lightning to the ambulance. Hector jumped on the bike and began to pedal. The blades that once held an umbrella together were spinning up in the air. The helicopter went high into the sky, carrying the ambulance with it. As they went over the cars, Hector shouted to the people below.\n\nHector: Carpools reduce traffic jams, and save fossil fuels. You can also take the train or the subway.\n\nPeople: Thank you, Hector.\n\nNarrator: After he dropped off the ambulance at the hospital, Hector rode Lightning to the big company. Elevators and escalators were everywhere, carrying people up and down.\n\nMr. Big: Thank you for coming, Hector. My company needs lights and elevators, but they use a lot of energy.\n\nHector: Let's go to the roof, Mr. Big.\n\nMr. Big: But there's nothing on the roof.\n\nHector: Exactly.\n\nNarrator: The roof was hot and sunny.\n\nHector: The sun gives us lots of heat and energy. You can put solar panels on your roof. They'll make electricity to help run your lights and elevators. Solar water heaters can give your company hot water for washing and cleaning. You can be an energy saver too.\n\nMr. Big: Excellent ideas, Hector. You're a hero.\n\nHector: I'm just happy to help our planet. And now I must go to help others save energy. Goodbye, Mr. Big.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9a8b631a91cd1c4c2541.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/7ab35180f3d9c0a25e51.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/24f2213115bc01a83c6a.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/944c0daa372ccdf78464.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Mom"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f5d75f195653dfbb974e.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f8fbe6ebab7a88c7fe25.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Mr. Big"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6c146aced23838761426.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/7e091f83007710919bbd.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1609691de2d67b42628f.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d231dcc1b8bcdc56feb0.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5fda2ac56f6f46245542.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Husband"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ff70d2629a552ae5ca69.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2e1a53a9994a9d4e8461.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/33a2d6df2520036f9eff.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/38ed2a9f0780c6c601f6.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"People"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3572c1c62de1ec292fe7.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5befa2cb96df86f84726.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Mr. Big"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ecf16978208cd0fe0bd6.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f6f3749eea6e007fb0a0.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Mr. Big"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/37fc074b36929d8646ef.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f27f7597767f23aee6a2.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/91c67960e4386eb87957.mp3","turn":21,"speaker":"Hector"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3240b985c146aefc8865.mp3","turn":22,"speaker":"Mr. Big"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/76ed3384c42c319329a9.mp3","turn":23,"speaker":"Hector"}],"Lisa (narrator): There is a poster on the wall at school. Surprise guest today at 2 o'clock in the gym.\n\nMarco: Lisa, who do you think it could be? I think it's an astronaut.\n\nLisa (narrator): You don't know who it is, I say.Nobody knows who it is because it's a mystery. Marco blows bubbles into his milk through a straw. I try hard not to laugh. He makes a face at me, and when I'm sure he's looking, I cross my eyes. I read in a magazine that if you close your eyes, you can hear things more clearly. If we close our eyes, maybe we can hear things in the next room, I tell Marco. The gym is next to the lunchroom, so I'm going to listen and find out who this special guest is. I close my eyes. I hear a low, deep booming sound, but I have no idea what it could be. It sounds like thunder, I tell Marco.Maybe it's a scientific machine for making thunderstorms or something.\n\nLisa: The mystery guest must be a scientist.\n\nMarco: I want to listen.\n\nLisa (narrator): He closes his eyes and tries to listen, too.\n\nMarco: I hear the sound of a loud trumpet call, Marco tells me with confidence. There is an elephant in the gym. The mystery guest is obviously a circus trainer.\n\nLisa (narrator): A trumpeting elephant is more deafening than that, I tell Marco. I like to use big words like trumpeting and deafening in front of Marco. I listen again.There is a humming, buzzing sound that makes me feel warm right down to my toes. I hear a hum, I exclaim.It sounds like lots of bees.The mystery guest is a beekeeper.\n\nMarco: No, it isn't, Marco declares.\n\nLisa (narrator): He closes his eyes even tighter.\n\nMarco: I'm positive this time, he tells me.I hear an enormous hungry cow. The mystery guest is a farmer and his giant cow.\n\nLisa: What are you talking about?\n\nLisa (narrator): There is no hungry cow noise.\n\nMarco: Yes, there is, Marco argues. He takes a deep breath and shouts,Mooooo!\n\nLisa (narrator): Suddenly the school bell rings and we both realize that it is already two o'clock. It's time to see the special guest. Marco and I race each other to the gym and look inside the door.\n\nMarco: Wow! This is so cool! Marco yells.How exciting!\n\nLisa (narrator): I agree, smiling. There is no machine that makes weather. No elephant, no bees, and no hungry cow. We see a man in a long fancy black jacket holding a thin white baton. All around him are musicians playing timpani drums, bassoons, cellos, trombones, and many more instruments.\n\nLisa and Marco: They're musical instruments! Marco and I say at the same time.\n\nLisa (narrator): The conductor hears us.\n\nConductor: He turns around and says,Welcome to the orchestra!":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/87e21977a9cf540349b8.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c60eb16912e23b567053.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/bb64a3b83a65df589471.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4782e26e381be45fa603.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Lisa"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/2774c25e74b18e7a06fc.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/311383cc939042e7b4a3.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e3c9fd6eb949113aa711.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f13ed5eb6f5d62a55071.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/d0866570c10bf8409a16.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/109fdc12fc11108cf66a.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8c7ba1c21e2550123d28.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/29fe3002684c63cf565d.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Lisa"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/c9361c1c8102e77683ed.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/40a4927366ca01c549ad.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/72424bb19f3b71beaf58.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b1c9e14a536bc37e46c6.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/eaa1cb8913b1db85647e.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/73c84daf1c25076777ac.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Lisa and Marco"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a4d1b94eaf4a20810095.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Lisa (narrator)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/13d7de0d645bfaa90821.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Conductor"}],"Narrator: Antonio's daughter Clara liked her father's workshop. She loved to look at all of the beautiful and colorful items he created. One day, she walked into the workshop and her father was there, standing beside two delicate glass vases. Many people in Venice knew who Antonio Rossi was. Like his father, Antonio was a very talented glassblower. He made many beautiful glass items, such as vases and water pitchers. With his new furnace, Antonio now made his popular glassware faster than before.\n\nAntonio Rossi: Do you like them, Clara? he asked proudly. I have made them for Mr. Lombardi, the great writer here in Venice.\n\nClara: They're beautiful, Father, Clara said.\n\nAntonio Rossi: I must go and get Mr. Lombardi, said her father.\n\nNarrator: Then he left the workshop. Clara looked at the vases. Beside them was a note.\n\nMr. Lombardi (note): Mr. Rossi, please make two beautiful vases for my wife, S. Lombardi.\n\nNarrator: Clara read the note and smiled. As she turned, she bumped the vases.\n\nClara: No, she gasped.\n\nNarrator: She tried to catch them, but it was too late. They fell to the ground and broke into many pieces. Only the round bases at the bottom were not broken. Clara reached down and picked up one of the round bases.\n\nClara: What will I tell Father? she thought.\n\nNarrator: She held the piece in front of her as big wet tears fell down her cheeks. It was then that she saw Mr. Lombardi's note as she looked through the glass. The letters were big. Clara looked at the base. It was flat on one side and curved on the other. She picked up the other base and looked through it. It worked too. The words looked bigger. Clara thought quickly. She found a length of wire and pounded it flat, then carefully wrapped it around the two glass pieces. As Clara finished, she heard footsteps. Mr. Lombardi and her father walked in.\n\nAntonio Rossi: Now let me show you the… Clara!\n\nNarrator: Clara's father stopped and stared at the broken vases on the floor.\n\nClara: I'm so sorry, Father, Clara whispered.\n\nNarrator: Mr. Lombardi looked at the two pieces of glass in a wire frame in Clara's hand.\n\nMr. Lombardi: What do you have there? he asked.\n\nClara: I made it from the broken pieces, said Clara. Look through them.\n\nNarrator: She held up the note for him to see. Mr. Lombardi's eyes opened wide.\n\nMr. Lombardi: The words, they're big.\n\nNarrator: He looked at Clara's father.\n\nMr. Lombardi: My wife can't read my books because of her eyes. This will be my gift to her. It will be the greatest gift ever.\n\nNarrator: Clara's father smiled.\n\nAntonio Rossi: I don't know how you did it, Clara, he said,but you made something very useful.\n\nNarrator: Clara had made something very useful indeed. She had invented eyeglasses.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/0912f504a0af96fa09e5.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/8f92be00d587684e52bb.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Antonio Rossi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/84e403b3f97812e0f050.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Clara"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/049bf9ee3cbf88d34d69.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Antonio Rossi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f59bf20616af6fc49929.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e815b37b508294a0238b.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Mr. Lombardi (note)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/282f8542f3a3aba29a93.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/78ace90a91b9f6fbbbe5.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Clara"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f557cc531f2bf6609577.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b3041cde45299b9c3138.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Clara"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e024544058886a43d888.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/751a55067ce5ec74c8ac.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Antonio Rossi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/audio/gap-cue.wav","turn":11,"speaker":"Antonio Rossi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b986f8c1fc06f25a3a2f.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Antonio Rossi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/b761a0e8b59a689c7182.mp3","turn":12,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/33ab8cdfe4db59ed5f37.mp3","turn":13,"speaker":"Clara"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/21174209b5a576b7c79f.mp3","turn":14,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4afa7f2781aae863a9bd.mp3","turn":15,"speaker":"Mr. Lombardi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/028661762ca34f578886.mp3","turn":16,"speaker":"Clara"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/623c974bcc202411006e.mp3","turn":17,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f05cad5235ff63c16296.mp3","turn":18,"speaker":"Mr. Lombardi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9b71451685e14f301f1e.mp3","turn":19,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/e927c4c846a789513379.mp3","turn":20,"speaker":"Mr. Lombardi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1710fad64bd19b39dcb8.mp3","turn":21,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fa4c401159717a01e78a.mp3","turn":22,"speaker":"Antonio Rossi"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3324c66826b8221ba0a1.mp3","turn":23,"speaker":"Narrator"}],"Narrator: A young girl named Marisol plants a single seed in the Garden of Happiness, a small community garden in her city. She waters it daily, and it grows and grows. Marisol is curious about her plant. What kind of plant is it? Meanwhile, a group of teenagers wants to paint a mural nearby. They aren't sure what to paint. One morning, Marisol saw an amazing sight from halfway down the block. She ran the rest of the way. Standing higher than all the plants and vines in the garden was a flower as big as a plate. Her bud had turned into petals of yellow and gold.\n\nMrs. Anderson: A sunflower! Mrs. Anderson exclaimed as she pushed her shopping cart by. Reminds me of when I was a girl.\n\nNarrator: The flower was a glowing circle, brighter than a yellow taxi. A flower of sunshine, Marisol thought, the happiest plant in the Garden of Happiness. All summer long, it made the people on the street stop and smile. Soon the air became cool and crisp with autumn. Mr. Castro picked the last of his tomatoes. Mr. Singh carried away a basketful of beans. Mrs. Willie Mae Washington dug up orange potatoes.\n\nMrs. Washington: I can almost smell my sweet potato pie, she winked at Marisol. I'm going to save an extra big slice for a good little gardener, I know.\n\nNarrator: But something terrible was happening to Marisol's flower. Its leaves were turning brown and dry.\n\nMarisol: My flower of sunshine is sick, Marisol cried. What should I do?\n\nMrs. Washington: Oh, child, Mrs. Willie Mae Washington said. Its season is over. There's a time to bloom and a time to die.\n\nMarisol: No, I don't want my flower to die.\n\nMrs. Rodriguez: Micarino, don't cry, Mrs. Rodriguez said. That's the way of a garden. You must save the seeds and plant again next spring.\n\nNarrator: Marisol was too sad to go to the empty lot anymore. For a whole week she couldn't even look down the block where her beautiful flower used to be. Then one day she heard people calling her name.\n\nNeighbors: Marisol, come quick! Marisol, apurate, hurry!\n\nNarrator: A golden haze shone on the street. There was a big crowd, like on a holiday. Music from the bodega was loud and bright, and what she saw made Marisol laugh and dance and clap her hands.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/eddaafa01ff3df50ebe1.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3c59218748890cd22526.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Mrs. Anderson"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9b4b6a77d693249ef34c.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/71f9486f63e67e698990.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Mrs. Washington"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/1465a68db364fd1d3449.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/6175dc9d1c1f4a04f777.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Marisol"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/95c04b070512272c4dad.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Mrs. Washington"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/469263f413e15446f9b1.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Marisol"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5b091253a32d50fbfadc.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Mrs. Rodriguez"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/5eca777eec3f30952a59.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f63978cf24b125f67e55.mp3","turn":10,"speaker":"Neighbors"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/67b621689eef5594380f.mp3","turn":11,"speaker":"Narrator"}],"Narrator: Juan is a ten-year-old boy traveling on Ferdinand Magellan's ship, the Trinidad. Magellan is an explorer from Portugal who wants to sail west to find a new route to Indonesia. Europeans go to Indonesia to trade for spices. Juan writes about his trip in his journal.\n\nJuan (journal): September 20, 1519. We sail from Spain today with 250 men and five ships. The sky is blue and the clouds are white. I'm not afraid. Captain Magellan is a brave man. I brought him his dinner of salted beef and beans. He gave me a coin.\n\nNarrator: The five ships sail towards South America. The journey is long. There are many storms with thunder and lightning. In November, the ships can't move because there is no wind to fill the sails.\n\nJuan (journal): November 10, 1519. The ocean is different today. It looks like glass. Every day, Captain Magellan says,Juan, go up to the deck and check the sails. I always go up and look around and bring back the same news. There is no wind.\n\nNarrator: The ships reach the coast of South America in December 1519. They stay there for one year. Then they sail through a narrow area of water called a strait and reach the Pacific Ocean. They lose one ship in a storm. Another ship sails back to Spain.\n\nJuan (journal): November 27, 1520. We are in a new ocean. Captain Magellan calls it the Pacific Ocean. I am sad to leave this land. Winter was cold and windy with fog and hail, but I saw many rivers and beautiful waterfalls. Now it is spring. It is time to sail west again.\n\nNarrator: The trip is long. There is very little food and water. The men are not healthy and some men die. The three ships reach the island of Guam on March 6, 1521. Then they sail west to other islands.\n\nJuan (journal): April 27, 1521. Captain Magellan died today in a battle on the island of Mactan. We have lost a great man, but I must be strong. We must get back to Spain.\n\nNarrator: Two ships remain. The men get to Indonesia on November 6, 1521. They fill the ships with spices. The Trinidad is damaged and can't sail. Only one ship sails back to Spain. Over 250 men started the journey, but only 18 men come home.\n\nJuan (journal): September 6, 1522. We are home today, after three long years. I am now 13 years old. I will never forget Captain Magellan. I really looked up to him. I have sailed around the world. I have seen many new places and learned so much. I am excited to tell everyone about my travels.":[{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/defa566289b4b05f2667.mp3","turn":0,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/9a3f693911efef384a5f.mp3","turn":1,"speaker":"Juan (journal)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/ace978d29886c78fcbc7.mp3","turn":2,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/f7a66fd5123e1433da3d.mp3","turn":3,"speaker":"Juan (journal)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/4ab69d58eac5854a5d3f.mp3","turn":4,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/80dc41e5ddacfc0fb6a1.mp3","turn":5,"speaker":"Juan (journal)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/3139d444a9b5134fc882.mp3","turn":6,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/746c1890f7ad7d41949c.mp3","turn":7,"speaker":"Juan (journal)"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/fc57897608a481e25419.mp3","turn":8,"speaker":"Narrator"},{"src":"../game-kit/adventures/dialogue-audio/a3baba37841cb7f948b7.mp3","turn":9,"speaker":"Juan (journal)"}]}}